From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nuno407 <11352538+nuno407@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 11:44:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 18/45] media: tegra-vde: allocate bitstream buffers
 write-combined

With the IOMMU in use the driver allocates all buffers with
vb2_dma_sg_memops, i.e. cacheable pages. Bitstream buffers are at least
2 MiB while a frame only fills a few KiB of them, yet both the dma-buf
end_cpu_access (DMA_BUF_IOCTL_SYNC, issued by GStreamer) and the vb2
buffer prepare clean the whole buffer, twice per frame. On Tegra30 with
the L2C-310 this made the kernel spend ~60% of the decode CPU time in
v7_dma_clean_range/l2c210_clean_range.

Allocate the OUTPUT queue with vb2_dma_contig_memops and
DMA_ATTR_WRITE_COMBINE instead and map it into the VDE IOMMU domain, so no
cache maintenance is needed. The buffers are small enough for CMA.

Measured with GStreamer v4l2slh264dec ! fakevideosink, 300 frames,
system CPU time: 1080p 7.58 s -> 2.86 s, 720p 2.52 s -> 1.25 s,
360p 1.66 s -> 0.37 s; output is bit-exact with avdec_h264.

Note that OUTPUT dma-bufs imported by userspace must now be contiguous
for vb2-dma-contig; GStreamer and FFmpeg use MMAP bitstream buffers.
---
 .../media/platform/nvidia/tegra-vde/v4l2.c    | 34 +++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 32 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/nvidia/tegra-vde/v4l2.c b/drivers/media/platform/nvidia/tegra-vde/v4l2.c
index f2ca1c7ed..b4b2811e2 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/nvidia/tegra-vde/v4l2.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/nvidia/tegra-vde/v4l2.c
@@ -187,7 +187,30 @@ static int tegra_buf_init(struct vb2_buffer *vb)
 			continue;
 		}
 
-		if (vde->domain) {
+		if (vde->domain && V4L2_TYPE_IS_OUTPUT(vq->type) &&
+		    vq->memory == VB2_MEMORY_MMAP) {
+			struct sg_table contig_sgt;
+
+			/*
+			 * Bitstream buffers are write-combined dma-contig
+			 * allocations whose kernel mapping is the DMA cookie.
+			 */
+			err = dma_get_sgtable_attrs(vde->dev, &contig_sgt,
+						    vb2_plane_vaddr(vb, i),
+						    vb2_dma_contig_plane_dma_addr(vb, i),
+						    vb2_plane_size(vb, i),
+						    vq->dma_attrs);
+			if (err)
+				goto cleanup;
+
+			err = tegra_vde_iommu_map(vde, &contig_sgt, &tb->iova[i],
+						  vb2_plane_size(vb, i));
+			sg_free_table(&contig_sgt);
+			if (err)
+				goto cleanup;
+
+			tb->dma_base[i] = iova_dma_addr(&vde->iova, tb->iova[i]);
+		} else if (vde->domain) {
 			sgt = vb2_dma_sg_plane_desc(vb, i);
 
 			err = tegra_vde_iommu_map(vde, sgt, &tb->iova[i],
@@ -346,6 +369,13 @@ static int tegra_queue_init(void *priv,
 	 * driver. For now we need to stick with SG ops in order to be able
 	 * to get SGT table easily. This is suboptimal since SG mappings are
 	 * wasting CPU cache and we don't need that caching.
+	 *
+	 * Bitstream buffers are an exception: they are at least 2 MiB while
+	 * only a few KiB are filled per frame, but cacheable SG buffers get
+	 * cleaned in full twice per frame (dma-buf end_cpu_access and buffer
+	 * prepare), costing several ms of CPU time per frame on Tegra30.
+	 * Allocate them as write-combined dma-contig buffers instead; they
+	 * are small enough for CMA and need no cache maintenance at all.
 	 */
 	if (vde->domain)
 		mem_ops = &vb2_dma_sg_memops;
@@ -362,7 +392,7 @@ static int tegra_queue_init(void *priv,
 	src_vq->requires_requests = true;
 	src_vq->lock = &vde->v4l2_lock;
 	src_vq->dma_attrs = dma_attrs;
-	src_vq->mem_ops = mem_ops;
+	src_vq->mem_ops = &vb2_dma_contig_memops;
 	src_vq->ops = &tegra_qops;
 	src_vq->drv_priv = ctx;
 	src_vq->dev = vde->dev;
-- 
